diff --git a/skills-v5/vector-search-systems/SKILL.md b/skills-v5/vector-search-systems/SKILL.md
index b6f5d566..2fca8c85 100644
--- a/skills-v5/vector-search-systems/SKILL.md
+++ b/skills-v5/vector-search-systems/SKILL.md
@@ -19,10 +19,27 @@ Own the storage and search mechanics around an already-defined vector representa
 8. **Measure index build and steady-state cost.** Record build duration, peak/resident memory, index/storage size, CPU/IO and any maintenance impact. A configuration that meets query latency but cannot be built, replicated or held in memory is not production-qualified.
 9. **Validate updates, upserts and deletes.** Define ID uniqueness/idempotency, visibility lag, tombstone/delete semantics, compaction/vacuum/rebuild needs and consistency expectations. Test that deleted vectors stop appearing within the stated freshness SLO and that concurrent updates do not create duplicates.
 10. **Quantize with measured reranking/recall tradeoffs.** Half precision, scalar/product/binary quantization or compressed indexes can reduce memory and latency, but compare against the uncompressed exact/reference results. If the engine expects reranking, include it in the measured query path.
-11. **Plan capacity and partitioning.** Model corpus growth, vector bytes, metadata/index overhead, replicas, hot working set and query concurrency. For sharded systems, verify routing/fan-out and global top-k merge semantics; for Postgres-style systems, consider partitioning/filter indexes when they improve selective queries.
+11. **Merge shards by exact score, then freshness.** Approximate scores from different shards are not comparable, so a distributed top-k is not a merge of the per-shard ANN scores. Take each shard's candidate set, rerank the union by the exact full-precision score for the query, and break ties by freshness, then cut to k. Verify that the global top-k equals what a single exact index would return for the same query, because that equality is the property a shard cut silently breaks.
+
+12. **Refuse a cutover the index cannot hold.** A rebuild that ranks correctly can still be the wrong index to promote: check that the new index can hold the projected corpus at the declared capacity before flipping traffic, and block the cutover when it cannot, reporting the shortfall rather than the ranking win. An overflow discovered after promotion is an outage with correct ordering.
+
+13. **Plan capacity and partitioning.** Model corpus growth, vector bytes, metadata/index overhead, replicas, hot working set and query concurrency. For sharded systems, verify routing/fan-out and global top-k merge semantics; for Postgres-style systems, consider partitioning/filter indexes when they improve selective queries.
 12. **Migrate indexes reversibly.** Build the new index/store from a versioned vector snapshot or dual-write stream, shadow the same queries, compare recall/filter correctness/latency/freshness, verify update catch-up and switch readers only after coverage. Keep the previous index until rollback is no longer required.
 13. **Monitor the search layer.** Track query latency/errors, returned-k shortfalls, recall canaries versus exact/reference search, filter selectivity, index freshness/build state, memory/storage and delete lag. A healthy service metric alone does not prove neighbor quality.
 
+## Reading the retrieval specification
+
+The task states its contract, and the contract decides the pipeline. Apply exactly the stages it names,
+in the order it names them: filter, preserve order, take k. Do not add a stage the specification does not
+ask for, and do not relocate one it does. A deduplication or aggregation step the task never mentions
+changes which ids are retrieved and moves recall, and it is not defensive engineering when the acceptance
+check compares against a literal reading of the contract.
+
+Where the specification is genuinely ambiguous, record the resolution next to the test that pins it, so a
+reviewer can see the ambiguity was a decision rather than a misreading. Where the input shows duplicates
+or an attractive aggregate that would change the unit of analysis, resolve it with the rule the task
+states, and if the task states none, keep the units the input defines and say so.
+
 ## Boundaries
 
 - `embedding-systems` owns provider/model/task/preprocessing/dimension/normalization representation identity and re-embedding migration. This skill owns how compatible vectors are stored, indexed, filtered and searched.
